{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass when you break loose (תריד).</b> Like תרדה, and they are two roots. And some say [it is] like \"I murmur in my complaint (אריד בשיחי)\" (Psalms 55:3), and the meaning is: when you pour out complaint, He will have mercy on you." "<b>And you shall break (ופרקת).</b> From the derivation מפרק הרים (1 Kings 19:11), and from them \"מפרקתו\" (1 Samuel 4:18). And there are in these blessings several difficult questions: if the blessing was prophecy, how did he not know whom he was blessing? And the discerning of the generation answered that HaShem said to him when Jacob came: bless this one. But if it were so, Isaac would have said to Esau: HaShem commanded me. And further, why did he bless Esau on account of his weeping? And others said that Isaac was not a prophet, and his blessing was not fulfilled — these too erred, for Scripture says \"And YHWH appeared to him\" (Genesis 26:2, 24), and David said: \"and do my prophets no harm\" (1 Chronicles 16:22). And others said: what utility is there in the words, since his intention was upon Esau, and the matter was in doubt for him, for thus he said: \"The voice is the voice of Jacob\" (Genesis 27:22), and he blessed whichever of them it would be, for they were both his sons. And some ask: how could HaShem say \"and the elder shall serve the younger\" (Genesis 25:23), [and yet] Isaac too said \"and your brother you shall serve\"? The Gaon [Saadia] rejected this question with a broken reed, because he found \"and he went to a land because of Jacob his brother\" (Genesis 36:6) — but he forgot \"and he bowed down to the ground seven times\" (Genesis 33:3). And the correct view in my eyes is that the blessing of a prophet is like a prayer, and HaShem heard his prayer, for the essence of this blessing concerns their seed. And sleepers who have not awakened from the sleep of folly will suppose that we are in the exile of Edom — but the matter is not so; rather, Edom was under the hand of Judah, and thus it is written: \"and Edom rebelled from under the hand of Judah\" (2 Kings 8:22), and Joab cut off every male in Edom (1 Kings 11:16). And because they were under the hand of Judah, they rejoiced on the day of our calamity, and they would say to the Babylonians: \"Raze it, raze it, to its foundation\" (Psalms 137:7). And the reproach, that Edom reproached Israel because of their suffering, was harder upon Israel; and thus the meaning of \"Rejoice and be glad, O daughter of Edom\" (Lamentations 4:21), and likewise \"do not rejoice over the children of Judah\" (Obadiah 1:12). Also in the days of Hyrcanus the elder — [when] the Shomronim [Samarians] of Jerusalem were desolate — he brought them into the covenant of circumcision. Also in the days of Agrippas, when Jerusalem was captured, bands of Edom came to help Judah. And the nation that exiled us is of the seed of Kittim, and thus the translator [Targum] said: \"and ships from the hand of Kittim\" (Numbers 24:24), and it is the kingdom of Greece itself, as explained in the book of Daniel. And there were people, few in number, who believed in a new faith, and as they believed in the days of Constantine who renewed the entire religion, and there was no one in the world who would keep the new religion except the Edomites. Likewise, today the people of Egypt and Saba and the land of Elam are called Ishmaelites, and among them there is none who is of the seed of Ishmaelites except a very few."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The phrase 'and they are two roots' refers to the two possible roots of תריד, which Ibn Ezra leaves unspecified.", 1 "In 'the Shomronim of Jerusalem were desolate,' the subject and syntax are ambiguous; translated hyper-literally. The phrase ושמם שומרי ירושלים is textually difficult — read here as 'the Shomronim [Samarians] of Jerusalem were desolate,' but the construction is compressed and possibly corrupt. The citation (מ\"ב ה כ\"ב) resolves to 2 Kings 8:22 by chapter numbering rather than the verse number 5:22 printed in some witnesses; the Hebrew chapter citation appears to follow a different numbering system."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:46:20.246896406Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}